Before writing the complete program, choose a small set of visible image anchors distributed across foreground, main subject and background. Record their approximate normalized positions or bounds in program comments, together with a few dominant edge directions and overlaps. Use the original image aspect throughout. These are visual estimates, not ground-truth calibration. Parameterize camera pose, target and lens separately from major asset dimensions and positions. Prioritize the contours and large light/dark regions that occupy the image; fine detail cannot repair incorrect framing or a wrong silhouette. 
Make these anchors operational in the complete program: choose four to eight explicit world-space points on identifiable visible geometry, such as room corners, board corners, a foreground tabletop corner or a fixture endpoint. Associate each with its estimated reference image coordinate (x right, y down, both normalized). Use points with clear correspondence; a group origin or world AABB corner is not automatically a visible feature. After realize and camera setup, update the view layer and project these points numerically using the actual camera matrix. camera.calc_matrix_camera(depsgraph, x=IMAGE_WIDTH, y=IMAGE_HEIGHT, scale_x=1, scale_y=1) preserves the supplied image aspect without changing runner render settings. Multiply by camera.matrix_world.inverted() and each homogeneous world point; for positive clip.w, normalized coordinates are (.5*(clip.x/clip.w+1), .5*(1-clip.y/clip.w)). Flag points behind the camera. Print one compact JSON table of anchor name, estimated reference coordinate, computed projection and residual. This is a geometry diagnostic, not a visibility test or image score. Before finalizing the program, use these projections to check framing and any predicted camera correction. If needed, evaluate a few small camera parameter perturbations numerically without rendering; compare their effects across anchors at different depths. Keep asset geometry fixed during that comparison, and restore the chosen camera before completion. Do not force uncertain anchors to exact agreement or deform the scene to fit incompatible estimates. A camera change should explain several residuals while preserving matching anchors. Local residuals may instead require asset placement or proportions. No extra renders, files or external calls are needed for this check.
